{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvpR23SrAcmTpnAZTu96","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QN5Afc5jTt6Ekq7D66mVce","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/workspace/paper/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\n\\title{Regression-Based Global Effect Estimation Under Network Interference: Exposure-Mapping Fidelity Governs Accuracy}\n\\author{Anonymous authors}\n\n\\newcommand{\\gate}{\\mathrm{GATE}}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nIn online experiments on social networks and marketplaces, a user's outcome depends on other users' treatments, and the quantity of interest is the global average treatment effect (GATE): the mean outcome difference between treating everyone and treating no one. Regression-based estimators fit an outcome model on exposure features from one randomized experiment and difference its predictions at the all-treated and all-control corners, but how accurate they are, and what determines their error, is poorly characterized. We study this on an eight-case synthetic grid (3000 nodes; stochastic-block, Barab\\'asi--Albert and Watts--Strogatz graphs; additive, strong, saturating, degree-dependent and two-hop interference; Bernoulli and cluster assignment), scored on 24 unseen seeds. A frozen estimator that chooses among 15 low-parameter exposure mappings with a BIC gate reaches a mean relative RMSE of 0.126, against 0.180 for plain linear exposure regression, 0.622 for difference-in-means, 0.994 for a neighbourhood Horvitz--Thompson estimator, and 0.045 for an oracle regression with the true mapping. Per-case error is 0.031--0.054 whenever the fitted mapping matches the generating form, whatever the graph family, interference strength (up to three times larger) or assignment design, and 0.151--0.452 when it does not; the two-hop case remains unresolved. Nominal 95\\% intervals cover only 0.839 of the time. The evidence is limited to one graph size, shared case specifications between model selection and final scoring, and single-factor case contrasts without per-case intervals.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nA/B tests on social networks and marketplaces violate the no-interference assumption: whether a user's outcome changes depends on who else is treated. The platform's decision-relevant estimand is then the \\emph{global average treatment effect} (GATE), the mean over units of the difference between the outcome when all units are treated and when all are untreated. The difference-in-means (DiM) estimator, which is unbiased without interference, is systematically biased for the GATE under interference, and design-based alternatives such as Horvitz--Thompson (HT) estimators of neighbourhood exposure \\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC,Ugander2013GraphCluster} avoid modelling assumptions at the price of large variance when the all-treated and all-control neighbourhood events are rare.\n\nA model-based alternative is \\emph{regression-based GATE estimation} \\citep{Chin2018RegressionAF}: fit a regression of outcomes on the unit's own treatment, a summary of its neighbours' treatments (an \\emph{exposure mapping}) and covariates; predict every unit's outcome under the all-treated and all-control assignments; and average the difference. This is attractive because it uses every unit rather than only those with extreme neighbourhoods. Its weakness is also visible in the construction: the two prediction points are the corners of exposure space (neighbour-treated fraction 1 and 0), which a randomized experiment with a moderate treatment probability rarely visits, so the estimate leans on the functional form of the fitted model. How accurate such estimators are in practice, which features of the mechanism, graph and design drive their error, and when they fail has not been laid out in a controlled grid.\n\n\\paragraph{Question and thesis.} We ask: in a single randomized network experiment, how accurately does a regression-based GATE estimator recover the truth, and which properties of the interference mechanism, graph and assignment design determine when it works? Our reading of the evidence is that \\emph{accuracy is governed by the fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping to the generating mechanism}, not by graph family, interference strength or cluster-versus-Bernoulli assignment within the tested range, and that the model-based intervals are miscalibrated regardless.\n\n\\paragraph{What we do.} We evaluate one frozen estimator, a BIC-gated library of parametric exposure mappings with linear regression as the fallback, together with four reference estimators (difference-in-means, plain linear exposure regression, a neighbourhood HT estimator and an oracle regression using the true mapping) on a fixed eight-case grid, using 24 seeds that were not used for model selection. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarizes the pipeline and the grid.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) A transparent, scale-free protocol (relative RMSE per case, averaged over cases) with four reference estimators and three negative controls. (ii) Measured accuracy of regression-based GATE estimation across five interference forms, three graph families and two assignment designs, with single-factor case contrasts that point to exposure-mapping misspecification as the dominant error source. (iii) Negative results: nominal 95\\% intervals cover 0.839 of the time, the two-hop case stays at relative RMSE 0.452 and its cause is unresolved, and two other estimator classes (a flexible ridge ensemble and a linear model with a design-weighted residual correction) did not beat plain linear regression on the model-selection seeds. We make no claim of a bound, a theorem, or superiority beyond this grid; Section~\\ref{sec:limits} lists what the evidence cannot support.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/overview_schematic.png}\n\\caption{Overview. A randomized assignment on a network yields exposure features (own treatment, neighbour-treated fraction or count, optional two-hop and degree terms); a fitted outcome regression is evaluated at the observed assignment and at the all-treated and all-control corners, and the average difference is the GATE estimate. The eight-case grid varies graph family, response shape, spillover range, strength and assignment design. Error can come from sampling noise or from misspecification of the exposure mapping, which matters most at the rarely observed corners.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Design-based estimation under interference.} Randomization-based frameworks define exposure conditions over a network and estimate their effects with inverse-probability weighting \\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC}; graph cluster randomization raises the probability that a unit's whole neighbourhood is treated together \\citep{Ugander2013GraphCluster,Ugander2020RandomizedGC}, and it has been deployed at scale \\citep{Karrer2020NetworkEA}. Subsequent work studies estimation theory under network interference \\citep{Sussman2017Elements}, the admissibility of HT estimation \\citep{Karwa2023Admissibility}, data-adaptive exposure thresholds for HT \\citep{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive}, interference with noisy or $k$-nearest-neighbour structure \\citep{Li2021Noise,Alzubaidi2023KNN}, and randomization tests for interference \\citep{Tiwari2024Quasi}. Our HT reference is of this family; it is included to show how variable a design-based estimator is at this sample size, not as a tuned competitor.\n\n\\paragraph{Designs for the GATE.} Several works change the assignment mechanism instead of the analysis: clustered and cluster-adaptive designs \\citep{Viviano2023CausalClustering,Zhou2020ClusterAdaptive,Nandy2019Dense}, staggered or two-stage rollouts \\citep{Cortez2024TwoStage}, designs matched to low-order outcome models \\citep{Eichhorn2024LoworderOA}, optimized covariance of the assignment vector \\citep{Chen2023OptimizedCD}, multi-arm designs under partial interference \\citep{Offer-Westort2021DesigningME}, experimental design with network information \\citep{Viviano2020Experimental}, and mixtures of randomized experiments \\citep{Jiang2023Mixture}. We hold the two designs fixed (Bernoulli and block-cluster) and study analysis only.\n\n\\paragraph{Regression adjustment and model-based estimation.} \\citet{Chin2018RegressionAF} proposed predicting the all-treated and all-control worlds from a fitted outcome model and analyzed it under linear models, with a companion central limit theorem \\citep{Chin2018Stein}. \\citet{Han2023ModelbasedRA} select graph-based and treatment-based covariates for the regression and use block bootstrap intervals. Generalized propensity-score approaches model exposure probabilities \\citep{Forastiere2022EstimatingCE}, and \\citet{Shankar2024ABTU} and \\citet{Bhattacharya2019Uncertainty} address estimation when the network is only partly known. Outside networks, regression adjustment is a variance-reduction tool in randomized experiments \\citep{Wager2016HighdimensionalRA,Ting2023Limits}. Our contribution relative to this literature is empirical: a controlled grid that separates cases where the fitted mapping matches the mechanism from cases where it does not, with explicit reference estimators, negative controls and an interval-calibration check. We do not derive new theory.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem setup}\nA graph $G$ on $n=3000$ nodes has adjacency $A$ and degree $d_i$. Each unit has covariates $x_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ and a binary assignment $z_i$ drawn by the design. Outcomes follow $Y_i(\\mathbf z)=\\mu_i(\\mathbf z)+\\varepsilon_i$ with $\\varepsilon_i\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1)$. The target is\n\\begin{equation}\n\\gate=\\frac1n\\sum_{i}\\big[\\mu_i(\\mathbf 1)-\\mu_i(\\mathbf 0)\\big].\n\\end{equation}\nAn estimator receives the graph, covariates, assignment, observed outcomes, the design description (kind, probability, cluster labels) and a nominal level of 0.95, and returns an estimate and an interval. It never sees the mechanism or the truth.\n\n\\paragraph{Metric.} The per-run error is relative, $e=\\min(|\\hat\\tau-\\gate|/|\\gate|,\\,2)$; instances are constructed so that $|\\gate|\\ge 0.5$. For each case the evaluator takes the RMSE of $e$ over seeds, and the plane score is the unweighted mean over the eight cases (``mean relative RMSE''; lower is better). We use this because GATE magnitudes differ across mechanisms and the cap of 2 keeps a high-variance estimator from dominating the average; a failed or invalid response counts as $e=2$. We also report the mean over cases of the absolute mean signed relative error (``bias''), the mean coverage of the 95\\% interval, and its mean width divided by $|\\gate|$ (capped at 4).\n\n\\subsection{Simulation grid}\nTable~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the eight cases; full parameters are in Supplementary Table~\\ref{tab:spec}. The outcome mean is $\\mu_i(\\mathbf z)=1+x_i^\\top\\beta+(\\tau_0+\\tau_1x_{i1})z_i+h_i(\\mathbf z)$ with $\\beta=(0.8,-0.5)$, $\\tau_0=1$, $\\tau_1=0.5$ and an interference term $h_i$ built from the neighbour-treated fraction $f_i=\\sum_jA_{ij}z_j/\\max(d_i,1)$:\n\\begin{itemize}\\setlength{\\itemsep}{0pt}\n\\item \\emph{fraction}: $h_i=\\gamma f_i$ with $\\gamma=1.5$ (cases 1--4) or $\\gamma=4.5$ (case 5);\n\\item \\emph{saturating}: $h_i=\\gamma/(1+e^{-10(f_i-0.5)})$ with $\\gamma=2.5$ (case 6);\n\\item \\emph{count$\\times$degree}: $h_i=0.15\\,c_i\\sqrt{10/\\max(d_i,1)}$, $c_i=\\sum_jA_{ij}z_j$ (case 7);\n\\item \\emph{two-hop}: $h_i=1.5f_i+2.0\\,f^{(2)}_i$, where $f^{(2)}_i$ is the treated fraction among nodes at distance exactly two (case 8).\n\\end{itemize}\nGraphs are a ten-block stochastic block model (SBM; expected within-block degree 14, between-block 6), a Barab\\'asi--Albert (BA) graph with $m=10$ and a Watts--Strogatz (WS) graph with $k=8$ and rewiring probability 0.1. Assignment is Bernoulli with $p=0.5$, or (case 2) five of the ten SBM blocks chosen uniformly and treated together. In SBM cases the first covariate carries a block-level shift. The graphs and truth are generated by an evaluator that is private to the estimator.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Eight-case grid and frozen-estimator error on the 24 unseen seeds (relative RMSE per case). Each factor contrast in the text is a single case pair.}\n\\label{tab:cases}\n\\begin{tabular}{clllc}\n\\toprule\nCase & Graph & Interference form & Assignment & Rel.\\ RMSE\\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & SBM & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli & 0.051\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel_case_1]]\n2 & SBM & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & cluster & 0.031\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel_case_2]]\n3 & BA & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli & 0.054\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel_case_3]]\n4 & WS & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli & 0.040\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel_case_4]]\n5 & SBM & fraction, $\\gamma=4.5$ & Bernoulli & 0.034\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel_case_5]]\n6 & SBM & saturating sigmoid & Bernoulli & 0.198\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel_case_6]]\n7 & BA & count$\\times$degree & Bernoulli & 0.151\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel_case_7]]\n8 & SBM & fraction + two-hop & Bernoulli & 0.452\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel_case_8]]\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Compared estimators}\n\\paragraph{Plain linear exposure regression (baseline).} Ordinary least squares of $y$ on $[1,\\,z,\\,f,\\,x]$, predicting at $z=\\mathbf 1,\\mathbf 0$ (with $f=1$ for non-isolated nodes at the all-treated corner and $f=0$ at the all-control corner). This is the canonical regression GATE estimator \\citep{Chin2018RegressionAF} with a one-hop fraction mapping; it matches the generating form in cases 1--5 up to the own-treatment-by-covariate interaction.\n\n\\paragraph{Frozen BIC-gated exposure library (studied estimator).} The studied estimator fits, by OLS, the linear model above plus 14 alternatives, each with few parameters: a treated-neighbour count; a two-hop walk fraction; interactions with log-degree and covariates (three variants); saturating $1-e^{-kf}$ with $k\\in\\{1,3,8\\}$; sigmoids of $f$ centred at $\\{0.25,0.5,0.75\\}$ with scale 0.1; and saturating functions of the count scaled by $\\{0.5,1,2\\}\\times$ the median degree. Exposure features are computed analytically at the observed, all-treated and all-control assignments. Count-type all-treated features are winsorised at three times the 99.5th percentile of their observed values. Each alternative is kept only if its BIC is lower than the linear model's by more than 10; kept alternatives are averaged with BIC weights, and if none passes, or the average departs from the linear estimate by more than three times $\\max(|\\hat\\tau_{\\mathrm{lin}}|,\\mathrm{se}_{\\mathrm{lin}})$, the linear estimate is returned. The interval is $\\hat\\tau\\pm\\big(1.96\\,\\mathrm{se}+|\\hat\\tau-\\hat\\tau_{\\mathrm{lin}}|\\big)$, where $\\mathrm{se}$ is the BIC-weighted mean of HC1 sandwich delta-method standard errors plus the weighted spread of the kept estimates. The estimator is deterministic and does not use the design description. It was written by inspecting the model-selection seeds; its per-case gate decisions were not logged in verified form, so we cannot report which mapping it selected in any case.\n\n\\paragraph{Reference estimators (evaluator-owned).} \\emph{DiM}: treated mean minus control mean. \\emph{HT}: a neighbourhood-exposure Horvitz--Thompson estimator that keeps units whose closed neighbourhood is entirely treated (or entirely control) and weights by the design-implied probability of that event. \\emph{Oracle}: OLS with the true exposure mapping for the case plus covariate and own-by-covariate terms; it is a reference for correct specification, not an attainable method.\n\n\\paragraph{Negative controls.} The evaluator also sends matched requests with permuted outcomes, a node-relabelled graph and a constant outcome, and replays one episode. These probe whether the estimator uses graph and outcome structure and whether it is deterministic.\n\n\\subsection{Protocol and scope}\nModel selection used eight seeds (101--108) on the same eight case specifications; three estimator classes were each scored once there (Supplementary Table~\\ref{tab:search}). One estimator was then frozen and evaluated once on 24 seeds (9001--9024) with fresh graphs and assignments, which is the evidence for all headline numbers. No invalid or failed runs occurred on the final plane. The final evaluation completed with outcome \\emph{qualified}; this means the evaluator's declared requirements were met on this grid and does not by itself establish validity, generality or superiority beyond it. Because case specifications are shared between model selection and final scoring, the final gain over the linear baseline measures behaviour on new seeds of known mechanism families, not transfer to new mechanisms.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Accuracy and baselines}\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/main_accuracy.pdf}\n\\caption{Final plane, 24 unseen seeds. (a) Mean relative RMSE over the eight cases for the oracle, the frozen estimator, linear exposure regression, difference-in-means (DiM) and neighbourhood HT (log axis). (b) Frozen-estimator relative RMSE per case; dashed lines are the oracle and linear plane means. Per-case values for the reference estimators are not available.}\n\\label{fig:main}\n\\end{figure}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:main}a gives the headline comparison. The frozen estimator attains a mean relative RMSE of 0.126 % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel]]\n versus 0.180 for plain linear exposure regression % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_linear_rmse_rel]]\n(ratio 0.701) % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.ratio_to_linear_baseline]]\n and 0.622 for DiM % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_dim_rmse_rel]]\n(ratio 0.203). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.ratio_to_dim_baseline]]\n The neighbourhood HT estimator averages 0.994 % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_ht_rmse_rel]]\n over the capped relative-error scale, which at $n=3000$ is not usable: few units have fully treated or fully control neighbourhoods when degrees are about 10 or more, and the unbiased estimate is dominated by noise. The oracle regression reaches 0.045, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_oracle_rmse_rel]]\n so the frozen estimator remains 2.8 times above the correct-specification reference. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#frozen_over_oracle]]\n The ordering oracle $<$ frozen $<$ linear $\\ll$ DiM $<$ HT is a plane-level statement: per-case values for the baselines were not recorded, so we cannot say in which cases the linear baseline loses to the frozen estimator.\n\nTwo aspects need qualification. First, the gap to the linear baseline is 0.054 on a plane mean, % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#frozen_vs_linear_abs_gap]]\n and the per-seed errors have a spread (Section~\\ref{sec:reliability}) that is comparable to a fraction of it; we did not run a paired test. Second, the frozen estimator, the baselines and the oracle all run on the same 24 instances per case, so the comparison is paired by instance but the number of seeds is modest.\n\n\\subsection{Where the error concentrates: exposure-mapping fidelity}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:main}b and Table~\\ref{tab:cases} show that per-case error is bimodal. In the five cases whose generating form is a linear function of the one-hop neighbour fraction (cases 1--5), the frozen estimator's relative RMSE lies between 0.031 and 0.054. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#cases_1_to_5_range]]\n In the three cases where the mechanism departs from that form it is 0.198 (saturating), 0.151 (count$\\times$degree) and 0.452 (two-hop). We read single-factor contrasts as follows, each from one case pair, with the caveat that no per-case interval exists:\n\\begin{itemize}\\setlength{\\itemsep}{1pt}\n\\item \\textbf{Assignment design} (case 1 vs.\\ 2, same graph and mechanism): 0.051 vs.\\ 0.031. Cluster assignment did not degrade accuracy; it also changes which exposure values are observed, so a difference of 0.020 should not be over-read.\n\\item \\textbf{Graph family} (cases 1, 3, 4; same form): SBM 0.051, BA 0.054, WS 0.040. No degradation for the heavy-tailed BA graph or the near-regular WS graph.\n\\item \\textbf{Interference strength} (case 1 vs.\\ 5): tripling $\\gamma$ from 1.5 to 4.5 gave 0.051 vs.\\ 0.034. Relative error did not grow with strength in this range; because the error is relative to a GATE that grows with $\\gamma$, a form-correct regression's relative error can shrink as the signal grows.\n\\item \\textbf{Response shape} (case 1 vs.\\ 6): the saturating mapping raises error to 0.198. The pair also differs in $\\gamma$ (1.5 vs.\\ 2.5), so shape is not isolated.\n\\item \\textbf{Degree dependence} (case 3 vs.\\ 7, both BA): 0.054 vs.\\ 0.151, a change in mechanism form on the same graph family.\n\\item \\textbf{Spillover range} (case 1 vs.\\ 8): adding a two-hop term gives 0.452, the largest error of the grid and about 2.3 times that of the next worst case. The pair differs in total interference as well as range.\n\\end{itemize}\nThese contrasts are consistent with the thesis that misspecification of the exposure mapping, not graph family, strength or assignment, is what moves the error, and they are inconsistent with a simple story in which heavier-tailed degree distributions or stronger spillovers alone degrade regression GATE estimation in this range. They do not prove it: the grid is not factorial, the cases that fail are also the three that depart from the linear fraction form, and each pair changes more than one property at a time in at least two instances. The oracle's plane mean of 0.045 is similar to the per-case errors in cases 1--5, which suggests that the residual error there is mostly sampling noise at $n=3000$, but this comparison is between a per-case value and a plane mean.\n\n\\subsection{Bias, variance and the gated library}\nThe mean absolute relative bias on the final plane is 0.074, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.bias_rel_abs_mean]]\n which is 0.588 of the mean relative RMSE. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#bias_over_rmse]]\n Because the per-case bias is not recorded, we can say that a substantial systematic component exists on average, not that it is concentrated in the failing cases; the model-selection plane suggests this (selected estimator: bias 0.092, RMSE 0.140) but is a separate plane. % @evidence[[results/ideas/03_parametric_exposure_mapping_family_with_bic_gated_switch_from_linear_regression_for_gate/program_1/metrics.json#bias_rel_abs_mean]]\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:search} (Supplementary) compares three estimator classes on the eight model-selection seeds. A flexible ridge ensemble with extrapolation-aware averaging scores 0.232 and a linear model with a design-weighted, shrunk corner-residual correction scores 0.185, against 0.184 for plain linear regression on those seeds; the gated library scores 0.140. Neither added flexibility nor design-weighted residual correction beat plain linear regression, while the library's gain came mainly from the saturating case (0.708 to 0.267 relative to the linear-based correction estimator) and was partly offset elsewhere (count$\\times$degree 0.183 versus 0.098 for the correction estimator). The two-hop case stayed at 0.445 under both linear-based estimators and the library on the model-selection plane, and at 0.452 on the final plane. The library did contain a two-hop walk-fraction mapping; why it did not resolve case 8 is \\emph{unknown}. Candidate explanations that we did not test are that the gate never fired, that the walk-normalized two-hop feature, which counts length-two walks that return to the node, differs from the generating distance-two fraction, and that the all-treated corner is poorly constrained. These are hypotheses only: gate diagnostics were not verified, and no ablation of the gate margin or library components was run.\n\n\\subsection{Reliability: seed variability and interval coverage}\n\\label{sec:reliability}\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.9\\textwidth]{figures/reliability.pdf}\n\\caption{(a) Per-seed mean relative error over the eight cases for the 24 final seeds, with the mean and a 95\\% percentile-bootstrap interval for the mean (10{,}000 resamples over seeds). (b) Mean 95\\%-interval coverage over cases against the nominal level, with mean relative interval width.}\n\\label{fig:rel}\n\\end{figure}\nPer-seed errors (Figure~\\ref{fig:rel}a) have a mean of 0.102 with bootstrap interval [0.093, 0.113]; % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#seed_mean]]\n this is the mean absolute relative error over cases, which is below the RMSE aggregate of 0.126 because RMSE weights large errors such as those in case 8. Two seeds stand out (9001 and 9015, with per-seed errors 0.180 and 0.173), while every other seed lies between 0.078 and 0.119; % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#outlier_seeds]]\n excluding them the mean is 0.095. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#mean_excluding_outliers]]\n Per-case decomposition for those seeds is not available, so we do not know which case drove them.\n\nThe nominal 95\\% intervals covered the truth at a rate of 0.839 averaged over cases, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.ci_coverage]]\n with mean relative width 0.379 % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.ci_width_rel]]\n (Figure~\\ref{fig:rel}b). This is undercoverage by 0.111 % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#coverage_shortfall]]\n on the plane mean. The interval is built from HC1 sandwich errors for a fixed library, widened by the departure from the linear estimate; it does not model selection uncertainty beyond that spread, ignores cluster dependence under the cluster design and uses no resampling. We report the undercoverage as an observed outcome; per-case coverage is not recorded, and we do not attribute the shortfall to a specific cause.\n\n\\subsection{Validity checks}\nThe constant-outcome control returned estimates with maximum absolute value $9.0\\times10^{-15}$, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_constant_max_abs_est]]\n so the estimator does not create effects from nothing. With permuted outcomes the mean absolute estimate was 0.059 of the true GATE, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_permuted_outcome_mean_abs_rel_est]]\n and with a node-relabelled graph the relative RMSE was 0.627, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_permuted_graph_rmse_rel]]\n close to DiM's 0.622: once the exposure features are scrambled, regression recovers only the direct effect, which indicates that the estimator is using the real graph. No control was vacuous, the replayed episode agreed exactly, and no episode failed. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.replay_abs_diff]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limits}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} On this grid, regression GATE estimation with a correctly shaped exposure mapping is accurate (3--5\\% relative RMSE per case at $n=3000$) across the three graph families, a threefold change in interference strength and both assignment designs. When the mapping is misspecified, error rises by a factor of three to ten. Because these are the tested regime, we do not extrapolate to other sizes, densities or mechanisms. Practically, the results suggest allocating effort to the exposure mapping, for instance with model comparison among a small family, before investing in the graph or the design.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Case specifications are shared between model selection and final scoring, and the library was designed after seeing model-selection per-case errors; the final result is out-of-sample over seeds but not over mechanisms. (2) There is a single graph size ($n=3000$) and a single assignment probability (0.5). (3) Contrasts are single case pairs, not a factorial design; they are not accompanied by per-case intervals, and several pairs vary more than one property. (4) Per-case values for the baselines, the bias, and the coverage are unavailable, so we make no per-case baseline comparison. (5) The cause of the two-hop failure and of the undercoverage is not established. (6) There was no ablation of the gate margin or library components, so we cannot attribute the gain over linear regression to any particular mapping or to the gate itself; only three estimator classes were scored once on the model-selection seeds. (7) All mechanisms are synthetic and known up to family; we ran no experiment on public graphs such as Facebook100 or ego-Facebook, none on real outcomes, and none with learned graph representations \\citep[e.g.,][]{Kipf2016GCN} or designs other than the two above. (8) Qualification of the final evaluation is a statement about the measured result on this grid.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe measured how well a regression-based GATE estimator recovers the truth under network interference on an eight-case synthetic grid. With a fitted exposure mapping that matches the mechanism, relative RMSE was about 3--5\\% per case, regardless of graph family, a threefold increase in interference strength, or cluster versus Bernoulli assignment. When the mapping did not match (saturating, degree-dependent and two-hop responses), error rose to 0.151--0.452. A BIC-gated library of parametric mappings lowered mean relative RMSE to 0.126 from 0.180 for plain linear regression, but remained 2.8 times above an oracle with the true mapping, did not fix the two-hop case, and produced intervals that cover only 0.839 of the time. These findings are limited to one size, one treatment probability, case pairs without per-case intervals, and shared case specifications between model selection and final scoring. The most direct next steps are a factorial extension over size, density and treatment probability with per-case intervals, an ablation of the gate and library, diagnostics for the two-hop failure, calibrated intervals (for example, cluster-aware resampling), and experiments on public graphs.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Simulation Design and Case Specifications}\nTable~\\ref{tab:spec} gives the parameters read from the case-specification file used on the final plane; model-selection seeds used the same eight specifications. Instances are generated from seeded random streams keyed by seed, graph family and node count. The evaluator requires $|\\gate|\\ge0.5$ and a 95\\% nominal interval level. Covariates are standard normal; in SBM cases the first covariate gets an additional block-level shift of scale 0.5.\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Case specifications (all cases: $n=3000$, $\\sigma=1$, intercept 1, $\\beta=(0.8,-0.5)$, $\\tau_0=1$, $\\tau_1=0.5$).}\n\\label{tab:spec}\n\\begin{tabular}{clll}\n\\toprule\nCase & Graph parameters & Interference parameters & Design\\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & SBM, 10 blocks, deg$_{in}$ 14, deg$_{out}$ 6 & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n2 & same as 1 & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & cluster, $p=0.5$\\\\\n3 & BA, $m=10$ & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n4 & WS, $k=8$, rewire 0.1 & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n5 & same as 1 & fraction, $\\gamma=4.5$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n6 & same as 1 & sigmoid, $\\gamma=2.5$, steepness 10, centre 0.5 & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n7 & BA, $m=10$ & count$\\times$degree, $\\kappa=0.15$, $m_{ref}=10$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n8 & same as 1 & fraction + two-hop, $\\gamma=1.5$, $\\gamma_2=2.0$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Estimator and Baseline Definitions}\n\\paragraph{Feature maps of the library.} With $f$ the one-hop treated fraction, $s$ the treated-neighbour count, $f^{(2)}$ the two-hop walk fraction (length-two walks from the node that start at a treated node, divided by all length-two walks from the node), $\\ell=\\log(1+d)$ centred, and $\\bar x$ centred covariates, the 15 mappings are: linear $[1,z,f,x]$; count $[1,z,s/\\bar d,x]$; two-hop $[1,z,f,f^{(2)},x]$; interactions $[1,z,f,x,\\ell,z\\ell,z\\bar x,f\\ell]$, $[1,z,f,x,\\ell,z\\ell,f\\ell]$ and $[1,z,f,x,f\\bar x]$; saturating $[1,z,1-e^{-kf},x]$; sigmoid $[1,z,(1+e^{-(f-t)/0.1})^{-1},x]$; and saturating-count $[1,z,1-e^{-s/(k\\,\\mathrm{med}_d)},x]$. Source: the estimator source file of the frozen estimator, functions \\texttt{candidate\\_designs}, \\texttt{bic\\_gate} and \\texttt{\\_estimate}. All-treated values are the analytic corner values (for example $f=\\mathbf 1[d>0]$, $s=d$, $f^{(2)}=\\mathbf 1[\\text{walks}>0]$). The BIC is $n\\log(\\mathrm{RSS}/n)+p\\log n$ with $p$ the rank of the design.\n\n\\paragraph{Fallbacks.} If the linear fit fails or any step raises, the estimator returns linear OLS with an HC1 interval; a constant outcome returns the estimate 0 with a degenerate interval.\n\n\\paragraph{Reference estimators.} DiM, linear and oracle regressions follow Section~3; HT uses inclusion probabilities $p^{|N_i|}$ and $(1-p)^{|N_i|}$ for Bernoulli designs, with $|N_i|=d_i+1$, and a hypergeometric probability over blocks touched by the closed neighbourhood for the cluster design. The oracle features are $[1,z,x_1,x_2,zx_1]$ plus the true mapping ($f$; $f$ and $f^{(2)}$; the sigmoid with true centre and steepness; or $c\\sqrt{m_{ref}/d}$).\n\n\\subsection{Per-Seed Final Results}\nTable~\\ref{tab:seeds} lists the 24 final seeds. The per-seed value is the mean relative error over the eight cases; runtime per seed (four estimate calls per case) ranged from 9.1 to 10.9 seconds, with median 10.4. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#median_duration_seconds]]\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Final per-seed mean relative error over the eight cases (seeds 9001--9024).}\n\\label{tab:seeds}\n\\begin{tabular}{cccccccccccc}\n\\toprule\n9001&9002&9003&9004&9005&9006&9007&9008&9009&9010&9011&9012\\\\\n0.180&0.096&0.091&0.116&0.089&0.113&0.090&0.088&0.090&0.091&0.094&0.080\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\\midrule\n9013&9014&9015&9016&9017&9018&9019&9020&9021&9022&9023&9024\\\\\n0.086&0.078&0.173&0.086&0.108&0.105&0.093&0.090&0.119&0.103&0.096&0.084\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\nThe mean is 0.102, the standard deviation 0.026 and the median 0.092. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#seed_std]]\n\n\\subsection{Negative Controls and Determinism Checks}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Final-plane validity checks for the frozen estimator.}\n\\label{tab:controls}\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nCheck & Value\\\\\n\\midrule\nConstant outcome, max $|$estimate$|$ & $9.0\\times10^{-15}$\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_constant_max_abs_est]]\nPermuted outcomes, mean $|$estimate$|/|\\gate|$ & 0.059\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_permuted_outcome_mean_abs_rel_est]]\nNode-relabelled graph, relative RMSE & 0.627\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_permuted_graph_rmse_rel]]\nVacuous controls & 0\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_vacuity_violations]]\nReplay absolute difference & 0\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.replay_abs_diff]]\nMissing or failed episodes & 0\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.missing_or_failed_episodes]]\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\nThe constant-outcome control rules out spurious effects from the covariate and exposure terms, the permuted-outcome control shows the estimate collapses toward zero when outcomes are unrelated to assignment and graph, and the relabelled graph shows that the real topology matters. None of these supports accuracy on its own.\n\n\\subsection{Model-Selection Comparison of Estimator Classes}\nTable~\\ref{tab:search} reports the eight model-selection seeds (101--108) on the same eight case specifications. The three estimator classes were each scored once; the final estimator was frozen from this comparison, so these numbers are a selection set, not final evidence. The flexible ensemble fits ridge regressions on exposure features, selects candidates by cross-validation plus held-out extrapolation to extreme neighbour fractions and degrees, averages those within one standard error, and uses a design-resampling wild block bootstrap interval. The residual-correction estimator adds to the linear estimate a shrunk, degree-stratified HT correction of the residual at the corners.\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\\small\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n\\caption{Model-selection plane (8 seeds): relative RMSE per case, plane mean, absolute bias and coverage.}\n\\label{tab:search}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccccccccccc}\n\\toprule\nEstimator & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6 & 7 & 8 & Mean & Bias & Cov.\\\\\n\\midrule\nFlexible ensemble & .070 & .055 & .050 & .033 & .314 & .710 & .200 & .428 & .232 & .185 & .688\\\\ % @evidence[[results/ideas/01_extrapolation_driven_error_of_regression_based_gate_estimators_under_network_interference/program_1/metrics.json#rmse_rel]]\nLinear + correction & .082 & .036 & .058 & .029 & .022 & .708 & .098 & .445 & .185 & .164 & .672\\\\ % @evidence[[results/ideas/02_design_weighted_residual_correction_augmented_regression_for_gate_under_network_interference/program_1/metrics.json#rmse_rel]]\nBIC-gated library & .082 & .031 & .058 & .029 & .022 & .267 & .183 & .445 & .140 & .092 & .813\\\\ % @evidence[[results/ideas/03_parametric_exposure_mapping_family_with_bic_gated_switch_from_linear_regression_for_gate/program_1/metrics.json#rmse_rel]]\n\\midrule\nLinear baseline & \\multicolumn{8}{c}{n/a per case} & .184 & -- & --\\\\ % @evidence[[results/ideas/03_parametric_exposure_mapping_family_with_bic_gated_switch_from_linear_regression_for_gate/program_1/metrics.json#baseline_linear_rmse_rel]]\nDiM / HT / oracle & \\multicolumn{8}{c}{n/a per case} & .623/.925/.050 & -- & --\\\\ % @evidence[[results/ideas/03_parametric_exposure_mapping_family_with_bic_gated_switch_from_linear_regression_for_gate/program_1/metrics.json#baseline_dim_rmse_rel]]\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\nThe flexible ensemble is worst on cases 5 and 6 (0.314, 0.710), consistent with extrapolation to the corners being unstable when many flexible models are averaged, though we did not test this. The linear-plus-correction estimator tracks linear regression in the aggregate (0.185 vs.\\ 0.184). Intervals on the model-selection plane also undercover (0.688, 0.672, 0.813).\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility Notes}\nThe estimator source file, the evaluator that generates graphs, assignments, outcomes, baselines and controls, the case-specification files for both planes, and \\texttt{build\\_figures.py}, which reads the evidence registry and regenerates Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:rel} and the derived statistics (bootstrap with fixed seed 0), accompany this submission. Table~\\ref{tab:cases} and Table~\\ref{tab:spec} come from the final case-specification file and the evaluator's per-case metrics; Tables~\\ref{tab:seeds}--\\ref{tab:search} come from the per-seed evidence file and the per-plane metric files. Each final seed took about ten seconds on CPU. The final evaluation used seeds 9001--9024; model selection used 101--108. Software versions are recorded in the accompanying task requirements file and are not restated here. The overview figure in the main text is a schematic and contains no measured values.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLarge language models were used throughout this study. They proposed the three estimator classes compared on the model-selection seeds, wrote the estimator source code for them, produced the figures and analysis scripts, retrieved and screened the cited literature, and drafted this manuscript. All reported numbers come from a fixed evaluator program run on the estimator source files, and from analysis scripts that read those outputs; no number was produced by a language model without a corresponding file. The authors of the research direction did not verify the estimator's per-case behaviour beyond the recorded metrics, as noted in the limitations.\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4807,"cache_read_input_tokens":147911,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4807},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"96cff137-170e-47bc-8fe8-44e05d15df83","uuid":"e3f549a2-f27a-42a4-8ac7-26bbe3fec95c","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:25:07.555Z","request_id":"req_011CgvpQzJ2KKyktiAPGuMcP"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QN5Afc5jTt6Ekq7D66mVce","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /workspace/paper/paper.tex (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"96cff137-170e-47bc-8fe8-44e05d15df83","uuid":"a24b14d3-4678-43a2-98ad-fda6bfdf6e79","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:25:07.570Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/workspace/paper/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\n\\title{Regression-Based Global Effect Estimation Under Network Interference: Exposure-Mapping Fidelity Governs Accuracy}\n\\author{Anonymous authors}\n\n\\newcommand{\\gate}{\\mathrm{GATE}}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nIn online experiments on social networks and marketplaces, a user's outcome depends on other users' treatments, and the quantity of interest is the global average treatment effect (GATE): the mean outcome difference between treating everyone and treating no one. Regression-based estimators fit an outcome model on exposure features from one randomized experiment and difference its predictions at the all-treated and all-control corners, but how accurate they are, and what determines their error, is poorly characterized. We study this on an eight-case synthetic grid (3000 nodes; stochastic-block, Barab\\'asi--Albert and Watts--Strogatz graphs; additive, strong, saturating, degree-dependent and two-hop interference; Bernoulli and cluster assignment), scored on 24 unseen seeds. A frozen estimator that chooses among 15 low-parameter exposure mappings with a BIC gate reaches a mean relative RMSE of 0.126, against 0.180 for plain linear exposure regression, 0.622 for difference-in-means, 0.994 for a neighbourhood Horvitz--Thompson estimator, and 0.045 for an oracle regression with the true mapping. Per-case error is 0.031--0.054 whenever the fitted mapping matches the generating form, whatever the graph family, interference strength (up to three times larger) or assignment design, and 0.151--0.452 when it does not; the two-hop case remains unresolved. Nominal 95\\% intervals cover only 0.839 of the time. The evidence is limited to one graph size, shared case specifications between model selection and final scoring, and single-factor case contrasts without per-case intervals.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nA/B tests on social networks and marketplaces violate the no-interference assumption: whether a user's outcome changes depends on who else is treated. The platform's decision-relevant estimand is then the \\emph{global average treatment effect} (GATE), the mean over units of the difference between the outcome when all units are treated and when all are untreated. The difference-in-means (DiM) estimator, which is unbiased without interference, is systematically biased for the GATE under interference, and design-based alternatives such as Horvitz--Thompson (HT) estimators of neighbourhood exposure \\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC,Ugander2013GraphCluster} avoid modelling assumptions at the price of large variance when the all-treated and all-control neighbourhood events are rare.\n\nA model-based alternative is \\emph{regression-based GATE estimation} \\citep{Chin2018RegressionAF}: fit a regression of outcomes on the unit's own treatment, a summary of its neighbours' treatments (an \\emph{exposure mapping}) and covariates; predict every unit's outcome under the all-treated and all-control assignments; and average the difference. This is attractive because it uses every unit rather than only those with extreme neighbourhoods. Its weakness is also visible in the construction: the two prediction points are the corners of exposure space (neighbour-treated fraction 1 and 0), which a randomized experiment with a moderate treatment probability rarely visits, so the estimate leans on the functional form of the fitted model. How accurate such estimators are in practice, which features of the mechanism, graph and design drive their error, and when they fail has not been laid out in a controlled grid.\n\n\\paragraph{Question and thesis.} We ask: in a single randomized network experiment, how accurately does a regression-based GATE estimator recover the truth, and which properties of the interference mechanism, graph and assignment design determine when it works? Our reading of the evidence is that \\emph{accuracy is governed by the fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping to the generating mechanism}, not by graph family, interference strength or cluster-versus-Bernoulli assignment within the tested range, and that the model-based intervals are miscalibrated regardless.\n\n\\paragraph{What we do.} We evaluate one frozen estimator, a BIC-gated library of parametric exposure mappings with linear regression as the fallback, together with four reference estimators (difference-in-means, plain linear exposure regression, a neighbourhood HT estimator and an oracle regression using the true mapping) on a fixed eight-case grid, using 24 seeds that were not used for model selection. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarizes the pipeline and the grid.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) A transparent, scale-free protocol (relative RMSE per case, averaged over cases) with four reference estimators and three negative controls. (ii) Measured accuracy of regression-based GATE estimation across five interference forms, three graph families and two assignment designs, with single-factor case contrasts that point to exposure-mapping misspecification as the dominant error source. (iii) Negative results: nominal 95\\% intervals cover 0.839 of the time, the two-hop case stays at relative RMSE 0.452 and its cause is unresolved, and two other estimator classes (a flexible ridge ensemble and a linear model with a design-weighted residual correction) did not beat plain linear regression on the model-selection seeds. We make no claim of a bound, a theorem, or superiority beyond this grid; Section~\\ref{sec:limits} lists what the evidence cannot support.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/overview_schematic.png}\n\\caption{Overview. A randomized assignment on a network yields exposure features (own treatment, neighbour-treated fraction or count, optional two-hop and degree terms); a fitted outcome regression is evaluated at the observed assignment and at the all-treated and all-control corners, and the average difference is the GATE estimate. The eight-case grid varies graph family, response shape, spillover range, strength and assignment design. Error can come from sampling noise or from misspecification of the exposure mapping, which matters most at the rarely observed corners.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Design-based estimation under interference.} Randomization-based frameworks define exposure conditions over a network and estimate their effects with inverse-probability weighting \\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC}; graph cluster randomization raises the probability that a unit's whole neighbourhood is treated together \\citep{Ugander2013GraphCluster,Ugander2020RandomizedGC}, and it has been deployed at scale \\citep{Karrer2020NetworkEA}. Subsequent work studies estimation theory under network interference \\citep{Sussman2017Elements}, the admissibility of HT estimation \\citep{Karwa2023Admissibility}, data-adaptive exposure thresholds for HT \\citep{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive}, interference with noisy or $k$-nearest-neighbour structure \\citep{Li2021Noise,Alzubaidi2023KNN}, and randomization tests for interference \\citep{Tiwari2024Quasi}. Our HT reference is of this family; it is included to show how variable a design-based estimator is at this sample size, not as a tuned competitor.\n\n\\paragraph{Designs for the GATE.} Several works change the assignment mechanism instead of the analysis: clustered and cluster-adaptive designs \\citep{Viviano2023CausalClustering,Zhou2020ClusterAdaptive,Nandy2019Dense}, staggered or two-stage rollouts \\citep{Cortez2024TwoStage}, designs matched to low-order outcome models \\citep{Eichhorn2024LoworderOA}, optimized covariance of the assignment vector \\citep{Chen2023OptimizedCD}, multi-arm designs under partial interference \\citep{Offer-Westort2021DesigningME}, experimental design with network information \\citep{Viviano2020Experimental}, and mixtures of randomized experiments \\citep{Jiang2023Mixture}. We hold the two designs fixed (Bernoulli and block-cluster) and study analysis only.\n\n\\paragraph{Regression adjustment and model-based estimation.} \\citet{Chin2018RegressionAF} proposed predicting the all-treated and all-control worlds from a fitted outcome model and analyzed it under linear models, with a companion central limit theorem \\citep{Chin2018Stein}. \\citet{Han2023ModelbasedRA} select graph-based and treatment-based covariates for the regression and use block bootstrap intervals. Generalized propensity-score approaches model exposure probabilities \\citep{Forastiere2022EstimatingCE}, and \\citet{Shankar2024ABTU} and \\citet{Bhattacharya2019Uncertainty} address estimation when the network is only partly known. Outside networks, regression adjustment is a variance-reduction tool in randomized experiments \\citep{Wager2016HighdimensionalRA,Ting2023Limits}. Our contribution relative to this literature is empirical: a controlled grid that separates cases where the fitted mapping matches the mechanism from cases where it does not, with explicit reference estimators, negative controls and an interval-calibration check. We do not derive new theory.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem setup}\nA graph $G$ on $n=3000$ nodes has adjacency $A$ and degree $d_i$. Each unit has covariates $x_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ and a binary assignment $z_i$ drawn by the design. Outcomes follow $Y_i(\\mathbf z)=\\mu_i(\\mathbf z)+\\varepsilon_i$ with $\\varepsilon_i\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1)$. The target is\n\\begin{equation}\n\\gate=\\frac1n\\sum_{i}\\big[\\mu_i(\\mathbf 1)-\\mu_i(\\mathbf 0)\\big].\n\\end{equation}\nAn estimator receives the graph, covariates, assignment, observed outcomes, the design description (kind, probability, cluster labels) and a nominal level of 0.95, and returns an estimate and an interval. It never sees the mechanism or the truth.\n\n\\paragraph{Metric.} The per-run error is relative, $e=\\min(|\\hat\\tau-\\gate|/|\\gate|,\\,2)$; instances are constructed so that $|\\gate|\\ge 0.5$. For each case the evaluator takes the RMSE of $e$ over seeds, and the plane score is the unweighted mean over the eight cases (``mean relative RMSE''; lower is better). We use this because GATE magnitudes differ across mechanisms and the cap of 2 keeps a high-variance estimator from dominating the average; a failed or invalid response counts as $e=2$. We also report the mean over cases of the absolute mean signed relative error (``bias''), the mean coverage of the 95\\% interval, and its mean width divided by $|\\gate|$ (capped at 4).\n\n\\subsection{Simulation grid}\nTable~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the eight cases; full parameters are in Supplementary Table~\\ref{tab:spec}. The outcome mean is $\\mu_i(\\mathbf z)=1+x_i^\\top\\beta+(\\tau_0+\\tau_1x_{i1})z_i+h_i(\\mathbf z)$ with $\\beta=(0.8,-0.5)$, $\\tau_0=1$, $\\tau_1=0.5$ and an interference term $h_i$ built from the neighbour-treated fraction $f_i=\\sum_jA_{ij}z_j/\\max(d_i,1)$:\n\\begin{itemize}\\setlength{\\itemsep}{0pt}\n\\item \\emph{fraction}: $h_i=\\gamma f_i$ with $\\gamma=1.5$ (cases 1--4) or $\\gamma=4.5$ (case 5);\n\\item \\emph{saturating}: $h_i=\\gamma/(1+e^{-10(f_i-0.5)})$ with $\\gamma=2.5$ (case 6);\n\\item \\emph{count$\\times$degree}: $h_i=0.15\\,c_i\\sqrt{10/\\max(d_i,1)}$, $c_i=\\sum_jA_{ij}z_j$ (case 7);\n\\item \\emph{two-hop}: $h_i=1.5f_i+2.0\\,f^{(2)}_i$, where $f^{(2)}_i$ is the treated fraction among nodes at distance exactly two (case 8).\n\\end{itemize}\nGraphs are a ten-block stochastic block model (SBM; expected within-block degree 14, between-block 6), a Barab\\'asi--Albert (BA) graph with $m=10$ and a Watts--Strogatz (WS) graph with $k=8$ and rewiring probability 0.1. Assignment is Bernoulli with $p=0.5$, or (case 2) five of the ten SBM blocks chosen uniformly and treated together. In SBM cases the first covariate carries a block-level shift. The graphs and truth are generated by an evaluator that is private to the estimator.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Eight-case grid and frozen-estimator error on the 24 unseen seeds (relative RMSE per case). Each factor contrast in the text is a single case pair.}\n\\label{tab:cases}\n\\begin{tabular}{clllc}\n\\toprule\nCase & Graph & Interference form & Assignment & Rel.\\ RMSE\\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & SBM & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli & 0.051\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel_case_1]]\n2 & SBM & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & cluster & 0.031\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel_case_2]]\n3 & BA & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli & 0.054\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel_case_3]]\n4 & WS & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli & 0.040\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel_case_4]]\n5 & SBM & fraction, $\\gamma=4.5$ & Bernoulli & 0.034\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel_case_5]]\n6 & SBM & saturating sigmoid & Bernoulli & 0.198\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel_case_6]]\n7 & BA & count$\\times$degree & Bernoulli & 0.151\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel_case_7]]\n8 & SBM & fraction + two-hop & Bernoulli & 0.452\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel_case_8]]\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Compared estimators}\n\\paragraph{Plain linear exposure regression (baseline).} Ordinary least squares of $y$ on $[1,\\,z,\\,f,\\,x]$, predicting at $z=\\mathbf 1,\\mathbf 0$ (with $f=1$ for non-isolated nodes at the all-treated corner and $f=0$ at the all-control corner). This is the canonical regression GATE estimator \\citep{Chin2018RegressionAF} with a one-hop fraction mapping; it matches the generating form in cases 1--5 up to the own-treatment-by-covariate interaction.\n\n\\paragraph{Frozen BIC-gated exposure library (studied estimator).} The studied estimator fits, by OLS, the linear model above plus 14 alternatives, each with few parameters: a treated-neighbour count; a two-hop walk fraction; interactions with log-degree and covariates (three variants); saturating $1-e^{-kf}$ with $k\\in\\{1,3,8\\}$; sigmoids of $f$ centred at $\\{0.25,0.5,0.75\\}$ with scale 0.1; and saturating functions of the count scaled by $\\{0.5,1,2\\}\\times$ the median degree. Exposure features are computed analytically at the observed, all-treated and all-control assignments. Count-type all-treated features are winsorised at three times the 99.5th percentile of their observed values. Each alternative is kept only if its BIC is lower than the linear model's by more than 10; kept alternatives are averaged with BIC weights, and if none passes, or the average departs from the linear estimate by more than three times $\\max(|\\hat\\tau_{\\mathrm{lin}}|,\\mathrm{se}_{\\mathrm{lin}})$, the linear estimate is returned. The interval is $\\hat\\tau\\pm\\big(1.96\\,\\mathrm{se}+|\\hat\\tau-\\hat\\tau_{\\mathrm{lin}}|\\big)$, where $\\mathrm{se}$ is the BIC-weighted mean of HC1 sandwich delta-method standard errors plus the weighted spread of the kept estimates. The estimator is deterministic and does not use the design description. It was written by inspecting the model-selection seeds; its per-case gate decisions were not logged in verified form, so we cannot report which mapping it selected in any case.\n\n\\paragraph{Reference estimators (evaluator-owned).} \\emph{DiM}: treated mean minus control mean. \\emph{HT}: a neighbourhood-exposure Horvitz--Thompson estimator that keeps units whose closed neighbourhood is entirely treated (or entirely control) and weights by the design-implied probability of that event. \\emph{Oracle}: OLS with the true exposure mapping for the case plus covariate and own-by-covariate terms; it is a reference for correct specification, not an attainable method.\n\n\\paragraph{Negative controls.} The evaluator also sends matched requests with permuted outcomes, a node-relabelled graph and a constant outcome, and replays one episode. These probe whether the estimator uses graph and outcome structure and whether it is deterministic.\n\n\\subsection{Protocol and scope}\nModel selection used eight seeds (101--108) on the same eight case specifications; three estimator classes were each scored once there (Supplementary Table~\\ref{tab:search}). One estimator was then frozen and evaluated once on 24 seeds (9001--9024) with fresh graphs and assignments, which is the evidence for all headline numbers. No invalid or failed runs occurred on the final plane. The final evaluation completed with outcome \\emph{qualified}; this means the evaluator's declared requirements were met on this grid and does not by itself establish validity, generality or superiority beyond it. Because case specifications are shared between model selection and final scoring, the final gain over the linear baseline measures behaviour on new seeds of known mechanism families, not transfer to new mechanisms.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Accuracy and baselines}\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/main_accuracy.pdf}\n\\caption{Final plane, 24 unseen seeds. (a) Mean relative RMSE over the eight cases for the oracle, the frozen estimator, linear exposure regression, difference-in-means (DiM) and neighbourhood HT (log axis). (b) Frozen-estimator relative RMSE per case; dashed lines are the oracle and linear plane means. Per-case values for the reference estimators are not available.}\n\\label{fig:main}\n\\end{figure}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:main}a gives the headline comparison. The frozen estimator attains a mean relative RMSE of 0.126 % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel]]\n versus 0.180 for plain linear exposure regression % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_linear_rmse_rel]]\n(ratio 0.701) % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.ratio_to_linear_baseline]]\n and 0.622 for DiM % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_dim_rmse_rel]]\n(ratio 0.203). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.ratio_to_dim_baseline]]\n The neighbourhood HT estimator averages 0.994 % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_ht_rmse_rel]]\n over the capped relative-error scale, which at $n=3000$ is not usable: few units have fully treated or fully control neighbourhoods when degrees are about 10 or more, and the unbiased estimate is dominated by noise. The oracle regression reaches 0.045, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_oracle_rmse_rel]]\n so the frozen estimator remains 2.8 times above the correct-specification reference. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#frozen_over_oracle]]\n The ordering oracle $<$ frozen $<$ linear $\\ll$ DiM $<$ HT is a plane-level statement: per-case values for the baselines were not recorded, so we cannot say in which cases the linear baseline loses to the frozen estimator.\n\nTwo aspects need qualification. First, the gap to the linear baseline is 0.054 on a plane mean, % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#frozen_vs_linear_abs_gap]]\n and the per-seed errors have a spread (Section~\\ref{sec:reliability}) that is comparable to a fraction of it; we did not run a paired test. Second, the frozen estimator, the baselines and the oracle all run on the same 24 instances per case, so the comparison is paired by instance but the number of seeds is modest.\n\n\\subsection{Where the error concentrates: exposure-mapping fidelity}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:main}b and Table~\\ref{tab:cases} show that per-case error is bimodal. In the five cases whose generating form is a linear function of the one-hop neighbour fraction (cases 1--5), the frozen estimator's relative RMSE lies between 0.031 and 0.054. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#cases_1_to_5_range]]\n In the three cases where the mechanism departs from that form it is 0.198 (saturating), 0.151 (count$\\times$degree) and 0.452 (two-hop). We read single-factor contrasts as follows, each from one case pair, with the caveat that no per-case interval exists:\n\\begin{itemize}\\setlength{\\itemsep}{1pt}\n\\item \\textbf{Assignment design} (case 1 vs.\\ 2, same graph and mechanism): 0.051 vs.\\ 0.031. Cluster assignment did not degrade accuracy; it also changes which exposure values are observed, so a difference of 0.020 should not be over-read.\n\\item \\textbf{Graph family} (cases 1, 3, 4; same form): SBM 0.051, BA 0.054, WS 0.040. No degradation for the heavy-tailed BA graph or the near-regular WS graph.\n\\item \\textbf{Interference strength} (case 1 vs.\\ 5): tripling $\\gamma$ from 1.5 to 4.5 gave 0.051 vs.\\ 0.034. Relative error did not grow with strength in this range; because the error is relative to a GATE that grows with $\\gamma$, a form-correct regression's relative error can shrink as the signal grows.\n\\item \\textbf{Response shape} (case 1 vs.\\ 6): the saturating mapping raises error to 0.198. The pair also differs in $\\gamma$ (1.5 vs.\\ 2.5), so shape is not isolated.\n\\item \\textbf{Degree dependence} (case 3 vs.\\ 7, both BA): 0.054 vs.\\ 0.151, a change in mechanism form on the same graph family.\n\\item \\textbf{Spillover range} (case 1 vs.\\ 8): adding a two-hop term gives 0.452, the largest error of the grid and about 2.3 times that of the next worst case. The pair differs in total interference as well as range.\n\\end{itemize}\nThese contrasts are consistent with the thesis that misspecification of the exposure mapping, not graph family, strength or assignment, is what moves the error, and they are inconsistent with a simple story in which heavier-tailed degree distributions or stronger spillovers alone degrade regression GATE estimation in this range. They do not prove it: the grid is not factorial, the cases that fail are also the three that depart from the linear fraction form, and each pair changes more than one property at a time in at least two instances. The oracle's plane mean of 0.045 is similar to the per-case errors in cases 1--5, which suggests that the residual error there is mostly sampling noise at $n=3000$, but this comparison is between a per-case value and a plane mean.\n\n\\subsection{Bias, variance and the gated library}\nThe mean absolute relative bias on the final plane is 0.074, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.bias_rel_abs_mean]]\n which is 0.588 of the mean relative RMSE. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#bias_over_rmse]]\n Because the per-case bias is not recorded, we can say that a substantial systematic component exists on average, not that it is concentrated in the failing cases; the model-selection plane suggests this (selected estimator: bias 0.092, RMSE 0.140) but is a separate plane. % @evidence[[results/ideas/03_parametric_exposure_mapping_family_with_bic_gated_switch_from_linear_regression_for_gate/program_1/metrics.json#bias_rel_abs_mean]]\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:search} (Supplementary) compares three estimator classes on the eight model-selection seeds. A flexible ridge ensemble with extrapolation-aware averaging scores 0.232 and a linear model with a design-weighted, shrunk corner-residual correction scores 0.185, against 0.184 for plain linear regression on those seeds; the gated library scores 0.140. Neither added flexibility nor design-weighted residual correction beat plain linear regression, while the library's gain came mainly from the saturating case (0.708 to 0.267 relative to the linear-based correction estimator) and was partly offset elsewhere (count$\\times$degree 0.183 versus 0.098 for the correction estimator). The two-hop case stayed at 0.445 under both linear-based estimators and the library on the model-selection plane, and at 0.452 on the final plane. The library did contain a two-hop walk-fraction mapping; why it did not resolve case 8 is \\emph{unknown}. Candidate explanations that we did not test are that the gate never fired, that the walk-normalized two-hop feature, which counts length-two walks that return to the node, differs from the generating distance-two fraction, and that the all-treated corner is poorly constrained. These are hypotheses only: gate diagnostics were not verified, and no ablation of the gate margin or library components was run.\n\n\\subsection{Reliability: seed variability and interval coverage}\n\\label{sec:reliability}\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.9\\textwidth]{figures/reliability.pdf}\n\\caption{(a) Per-seed mean relative error over the eight cases for the 24 final seeds, with the mean and a 95\\% percentile-bootstrap interval for the mean (10{,}000 resamples over seeds). (b) Mean 95\\%-interval coverage over cases against the nominal level, with mean relative interval width.}\n\\label{fig:rel}\n\\end{figure}\nPer-seed errors (Figure~\\ref{fig:rel}a) have a mean of 0.102 with bootstrap interval [0.093, 0.113]; % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#seed_mean]]\n this is the mean absolute relative error over cases, which is below the RMSE aggregate of 0.126 because RMSE weights large errors such as those in case 8. Two seeds stand out (9001 and 9015, with per-seed errors 0.180 and 0.173), while every other seed lies between 0.078 and 0.119; % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#outlier_seeds]]\n excluding them the mean is 0.095. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#mean_excluding_outliers]]\n Per-case decomposition for those seeds is not available, so we do not know which case drove them.\n\nThe nominal 95\\% intervals covered the truth at a rate of 0.839 averaged over cases, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.ci_coverage]]\n with mean relative width 0.379 % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.ci_width_rel]]\n (Figure~\\ref{fig:rel}b). This is undercoverage by 0.111 % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#coverage_shortfall]]\n on the plane mean. The interval is built from HC1 sandwich errors for a fixed library, widened by the departure from the linear estimate; it does not model selection uncertainty beyond that spread, ignores cluster dependence under the cluster design and uses no resampling. We report the undercoverage as an observed outcome; per-case coverage is not recorded, and we do not attribute the shortfall to a specific cause.\n\n\\subsection{Validity checks}\nThe constant-outcome control returned estimates with maximum absolute value $9.0\\times10^{-15}$, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_constant_max_abs_est]]\n so the estimator does not create effects from nothing. With permuted outcomes the mean absolute estimate was 0.059 of the true GATE, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_permuted_outcome_mean_abs_rel_est]]\n and with a node-relabelled graph the relative RMSE was 0.627, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_permuted_graph_rmse_rel]]\n close to DiM's 0.622: once the exposure features are scrambled, regression recovers only the direct effect, which indicates that the estimator is using the real graph. No control was vacuous, the replayed episode agreed exactly, and no episode failed. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.replay_abs_diff]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limits}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} On this grid, regression GATE estimation with a correctly shaped exposure mapping is accurate (3--5\\% relative RMSE per case at $n=3000$) across the three graph families, a threefold change in interference strength and both assignment designs. When the mapping is misspecified, error rises by a factor of three to ten. Because these are the tested regime, we do not extrapolate to other sizes, densities or mechanisms. Practically, the results suggest allocating effort to the exposure mapping, for instance with model comparison among a small family, before investing in the graph or the design.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Case specifications are shared between model selection and final scoring, and the library was designed after seeing model-selection per-case errors; the final result is out-of-sample over seeds but not over mechanisms. (2) There is a single graph size ($n=3000$) and a single assignment probability (0.5). (3) Contrasts are single case pairs, not a factorial design; they are not accompanied by per-case intervals, and several pairs vary more than one property. (4) Per-case values for the baselines, the bias, and the coverage are unavailable, so we make no per-case baseline comparison. (5) The cause of the two-hop failure and of the undercoverage is not established. (6) There was no ablation of the gate margin or library components, so we cannot attribute the gain over linear regression to any particular mapping or to the gate itself; only three estimator classes were scored once on the model-selection seeds. (7) All mechanisms are synthetic and known up to family; we ran no experiment on public graphs such as Facebook100 or ego-Facebook, none on real outcomes, and none with learned graph representations \\citep[e.g.,][]{Kipf2016GCN} or designs other than the two above. (8) Qualification of the final evaluation is a statement about the measured result on this grid.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe measured how well a regression-based GATE estimator recovers the truth under network interference on an eight-case synthetic grid. With a fitted exposure mapping that matches the mechanism, relative RMSE was about 3--5\\% per case, regardless of graph family, a threefold increase in interference strength, or cluster versus Bernoulli assignment. When the mapping did not match (saturating, degree-dependent and two-hop responses), error rose to 0.151--0.452. A BIC-gated library of parametric mappings lowered mean relative RMSE to 0.126 from 0.180 for plain linear regression, but remained 2.8 times above an oracle with the true mapping, did not fix the two-hop case, and produced intervals that cover only 0.839 of the time. These findings are limited to one size, one treatment probability, case pairs without per-case intervals, and shared case specifications between model selection and final scoring. The most direct next steps are a factorial extension over size, density and treatment probability with per-case intervals, an ablation of the gate and library, diagnostics for the two-hop failure, calibrated intervals (for example, cluster-aware resampling), and experiments on public graphs.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Simulation Design and Case Specifications}\nTable~\\ref{tab:spec} gives the parameters read from the case-specification file used on the final plane; model-selection seeds used the same eight specifications. Instances are generated from seeded random streams keyed by seed, graph family and node count. The evaluator requires $|\\gate|\\ge0.5$ and a 95\\% nominal interval level. Covariates are standard normal; in SBM cases the first covariate gets an additional block-level shift of scale 0.5.\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Case specifications (all cases: $n=3000$, $\\sigma=1$, intercept 1, $\\beta=(0.8,-0.5)$, $\\tau_0=1$, $\\tau_1=0.5$).}\n\\label{tab:spec}\n\\begin{tabular}{clll}\n\\toprule\nCase & Graph parameters & Interference parameters & Design\\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & SBM, 10 blocks, deg$_{in}$ 14, deg$_{out}$ 6 & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n2 & same as 1 & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & cluster, $p=0.5$\\\\\n3 & BA, $m=10$ & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n4 & WS, $k=8$, rewire 0.1 & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n5 & same as 1 & fraction, $\\gamma=4.5$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n6 & same as 1 & sigmoid, $\\gamma=2.5$, steepness 10, centre 0.5 & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n7 & BA, $m=10$ & count$\\times$degree, $\\kappa=0.15$, $m_{ref}=10$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n8 & same as 1 & fraction + two-hop, $\\gamma=1.5$, $\\gamma_2=2.0$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Estimator and Baseline Definitions}\n\\paragraph{Feature maps of the library.} With $f$ the one-hop treated fraction, $s$ the treated-neighbour count, $f^{(2)}$ the two-hop walk fraction (length-two walks from the node that start at a treated node, divided by all length-two walks from the node), $\\ell=\\log(1+d)$ centred, and $\\bar x$ centred covariates, the 15 mappings are: linear $[1,z,f,x]$; count $[1,z,s/\\bar d,x]$; two-hop $[1,z,f,f^{(2)},x]$; interactions $[1,z,f,x,\\ell,z\\ell,z\\bar x,f\\ell]$, $[1,z,f,x,\\ell,z\\ell,f\\ell]$ and $[1,z,f,x,f\\bar x]$; saturating $[1,z,1-e^{-kf},x]$; sigmoid $[1,z,(1+e^{-(f-t)/0.1})^{-1},x]$; and saturating-count $[1,z,1-e^{-s/(k\\,\\mathrm{med}_d)},x]$. Source: the estimator source file of the frozen estimator, functions \\texttt{candidate\\_designs}, \\texttt{bic\\_gate} and \\texttt{\\_estimate}. All-treated values are the analytic corner values (for example $f=\\mathbf 1[d>0]$, $s=d$, $f^{(2)}=\\mathbf 1[\\text{walks}>0]$). The BIC is $n\\log(\\mathrm{RSS}/n)+p\\log n$ with $p$ the rank of the design.\n\n\\paragraph{Fallbacks.} If the linear fit fails or any step raises, the estimator returns linear OLS with an HC1 interval; a constant outcome returns the estimate 0 with a degenerate interval.\n\n\\paragraph{Reference estimators.} DiM, linear and oracle regressions follow Section~3; HT uses inclusion probabilities $p^{|N_i|}$ and $(1-p)^{|N_i|}$ for Bernoulli designs, with $|N_i|=d_i+1$, and a hypergeometric probability over blocks touched by the closed neighbourhood for the cluster design. The oracle features are $[1,z,x_1,x_2,zx_1]$ plus the true mapping ($f$; $f$ and $f^{(2)}$; the sigmoid with true centre and steepness; or $c\\sqrt{m_{ref}/d}$).\n\n\\subsection{Per-Seed Final Results}\nTable~\\ref{tab:seeds} lists the 24 final seeds. The per-seed value is the mean relative error over the eight cases; runtime per seed (four estimate calls per case) ranged from 9.1 to 10.9 seconds, with median 10.4. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#median_duration_seconds]]\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Final per-seed mean relative error over the eight cases (seeds 9001--9024).}\n\\label{tab:seeds}\n\\begin{tabular}{cccccccccccc}\n\\toprule\n9001&9002&9003&9004&9005&9006&9007&9008&9009&9010&9011&9012\\\\\n0.180&0.096&0.091&0.116&0.089&0.113&0.090&0.088&0.090&0.091&0.094&0.080\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\\midrule\n9013&9014&9015&9016&9017&9018&9019&9020&9021&9022&9023&9024\\\\\n0.086&0.078&0.173&0.086&0.108&0.105&0.093&0.090&0.119&0.103&0.096&0.084\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\nThe mean is 0.102, the standard deviation 0.026 and the median 0.092. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#seed_std]]\n\n\\subsection{Negative Controls and Determinism Checks}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Final-plane validity checks for the frozen estimator.}\n\\label{tab:controls}\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nCheck & Value\\\\\n\\midrule\nConstant outcome, max $|$estimate$|$ & $9.0\\times10^{-15}$\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_constant_max_abs_est]]\nPermuted outcomes, mean $|$estimate$|/|\\gate|$ & 0.059\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_permuted_outcome_mean_abs_rel_est]]\nNode-relabelled graph, relative RMSE & 0.627\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_permuted_graph_rmse_rel]]\nVacuous controls & 0\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_vacuity_violations]]\nReplay absolute difference & 0\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.replay_abs_diff]]\nMissing or failed episodes & 0\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.missing_or_failed_episodes]]\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\nThe constant-outcome control rules out spurious effects from the covariate and exposure terms, the permuted-outcome control shows the estimate collapses toward zero when outcomes are unrelated to assignment and graph, and the relabelled graph shows that the real topology matters. None of these supports accuracy on its own.\n\n\\subsection{Model-Selection Comparison of Estimator Classes}\nTable~\\ref{tab:search} reports the eight model-selection seeds (101--108) on the same eight case specifications. The three estimator classes were each scored once; the final estimator was frozen from this comparison, so these numbers are a selection set, not final evidence. The flexible ensemble fits ridge regressions on exposure features, selects candidates by cross-validation plus held-out extrapolation to extreme neighbour fractions and degrees, averages those within one standard error, and uses a design-resampling wild block bootstrap interval. The residual-correction estimator adds to the linear estimate a shrunk, degree-stratified HT correction of the residual at the corners.\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\\small\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n\\caption{Model-selection plane (8 seeds): relative RMSE per case, plane mean, absolute bias and coverage.}\n\\label{tab:search}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccccccccccc}\n\\toprule\nEstimator & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6 & 7 & 8 & Mean & Bias & Cov.\\\\\n\\midrule\nFlexible ensemble & .070 & .055 & .050 & .033 & .314 & .710 & .200 & .428 & .232 & .185 & .688\\\\ % @evidence[[results/ideas/01_extrapolation_driven_error_of_regression_based_gate_estimators_under_network_interference/program_1/metrics.json#rmse_rel]]\nLinear + correction & .082 & .036 & .058 & .029 & .022 & .708 & .098 & .445 & .185 & .164 & .672\\\\ % @evidence[[results/ideas/02_design_weighted_residual_correction_augmented_regression_for_gate_under_network_interference/program_1/metrics.json#rmse_rel]]\nBIC-gated library & .082 & .031 & .058 & .029 & .022 & .267 & .183 & .445 & .140 & .092 & .813\\\\ % @evidence[[results/ideas/03_parametric_exposure_mapping_family_with_bic_gated_switch_from_linear_regression_for_gate/program_1/metrics.json#rmse_rel]]\n\\midrule\nLinear baseline & \\multicolumn{8}{c}{n/a per case} & .184 & -- & --\\\\ % @evidence[[results/ideas/03_parametric_exposure_mapping_family_with_bic_gated_switch_from_linear_regression_for_gate/program_1/metrics.json#baseline_linear_rmse_rel]]\nDiM / HT / oracle & \\multicolumn{8}{c}{n/a per case} & .623/.925/.050 & -- & --\\\\ % @evidence[[results/ideas/03_parametric_exposure_mapping_family_with_bic_gated_switch_from_linear_regression_for_gate/program_1/metrics.json#baseline_dim_rmse_rel]]\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\nThe flexible ensemble is worst on cases 5 and 6 (0.314, 0.710), consistent with extrapolation to the corners being unstable when many flexible models are averaged, though we did not test this. The linear-plus-correction estimator tracks linear regression in the aggregate (0.185 vs.\\ 0.184). Intervals on the model-selection plane also undercover (0.688, 0.672, 0.813).\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility Notes}\nThe estimator source file, the evaluator that generates graphs, assignments, outcomes, baselines and controls, the case-specification files for both planes, and \\texttt{build\\_figures.py}, which reads the evidence registry and regenerates Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:rel} and the derived statistics (bootstrap with fixed seed 0), accompany this submission. Table~\\ref{tab:cases} and Table~\\ref{tab:spec} come from the final case-specification file and the evaluator's per-case metrics; Tables~\\ref{tab:seeds}--\\ref{tab:search} come from the per-seed evidence file and the per-plane metric files. Each final seed took about ten seconds on CPU. The final evaluation used seeds 9001--9024; model selection used 101--108. Software versions are recorded in the accompanying task requirements file and are not restated here. The overview figure in the main text is a schematic and contains no measured values.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLarge language models were used throughout this study. They proposed the three estimator classes compared on the model-selection seeds, wrote the estimator source code for them, produced the figures and analysis scripts, retrieved and screened the cited literature, and drafted this manuscript. All reported numbers come from a fixed evaluator program run on the estimator source files, and from analysis scripts that read those outputs; no number was produced by a language model without a corresponding file. The authors of the research direction did not verify the estimator's per-case behaviour beyond the recorded metrics, as noted in the limitations.\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
