{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvpeuSDUd1b2qDKoPV4s","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GBc8jHBpb33cEFWThQSfYm","name":"bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\nb=open('build_figures.py').read()\nb=b.replace('    \"bias_over_rmse\":','    \"oracle_over_linear\": float(M[\"baseline_oracle_rmse_rel\"] / M[\"baseline_linear_rmse_rel\"]),\\n    \"dim_over_linear\": float(M[\"baseline_dim_rmse_rel\"] / M[\"baseline_linear_rmse_rel\"]),\\n    \"ht_over_linear\": float(M[\"baseline_ht_rmse_rel\"] / M[\"baseline_linear_rmse_rel\"]),\\n    \"worst_over_next_worst_case\": float(M[\"rmse_rel_case_8\"] / M[\"rmse_rel_case_6\"]),\\n    \"bias_over_rmse\":')\nopen('build_figures.py','w').write(b)\n\ns=open('paper.tex').read()\nheur='''\\\\subsection{A heuristic account of the corner extrapolation}\nThe pattern in Table~\\\\ref{tab:cases} fits a simple account that we offer as interpretation, not as a tested mechanism. Least squares fits the outcome surface where the data are: with Bernoulli assignment at $p=0.5$, a node of degree $d$ has neighbour fraction $f_i$ concentrated near 0.5 with spread of order $\\\\sqrt{0.25/d}$, so observed exposures cover the middle of $[0,1]$ much more densely than the corners $f=0$ and $f=1$ at which the GATE is evaluated. If $h$ is linear in $f$, the fitted slope is the same everywhere and the corner prediction is correct up to sampling error. If $h$ is nonlinear, the fitted slope is a local average of $h'$ over the observed range and the corner contrast $h(1)-h(0)$ is replaced by that slope times the corner distance, which is wrong by an amount that depends on how $h'$ differs between the middle and the ends of the range. For the sigmoid in case 6 (centre 0.5, steepness 10) the slope is largest at the middle, exactly where the data lie, so a linear fit extrapolates a steep local slope to the corners. For the two-hop case, an exposure mapping that omits the second-hop term loses a component of the effect that is entirely absent from $f$, while for count$\\\\times$degree interference the bias depends on how the count scales with degree across nodes. Under this account, graph family and assignment design matter only through how well the observed exposure distribution covers the corners and through the noise level; cluster assignment widens exposure coverage toward the corners, which is a possible reason for the lower error in case 2, but we have no experiment isolating this. We did not measure corner coverage, so these statements remain hypotheses that the grid is consistent with.\n'''\na=\"\\\\subsection{Bias, variance and the gated library}\"\ns=s.replace(a,heur+\"\\n\"+a,1)\n\n# expand discussion\nold=\"Practically, the results suggest allocating effort to the exposure mapping, for instance with model comparison among a small family, before investing in the graph or the design.\"\nnew=\"Practically, the results suggest allocating effort to the exposure mapping, for instance with model comparison among a small family, before investing in the graph or the design. At the same time, a gate of this kind has costs: in the model-selection comparison, the library improved the saturating case but was worse than the linear-plus-correction estimator on the count$\\\\times$degree case, so a library is not uniformly better than a simpler fit, and the choice of margin and members is itself a source of researcher degrees of freedom that we did not ablate.\"\nassert old in s; s=s.replace(old,new)\nold2=\"\\\\paragraph{Limitations.}\"\nnew2=\"\\\\paragraph{Relation to design-based estimation.} The HT reference is design-based and unbiased for its exposure contrast when the exposure condition is well defined, whereas the regression estimators accept model risk in exchange for using every unit. In this grid at $n=3000$ the variance of the former was prohibitive and the model risk of the latter was moderate to large only when the mapping was wrong. This does not imply that regression dominates design-based or cluster-randomized approaches in general: we compared analyses under two fixed designs, did not tune HT (for instance with exposure thresholds as in \\\\citealp{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive}), and did not vary graph density or size, which control how often extreme exposure occurs.\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} The evaluator produces mechanisms whose forms the library partly anticipates (the library includes sigmoids centred at 0.5 and saturating and count-based shapes), so the gain over linear regression reflects a match between the library and the grid. A practitioner whose true mechanism lies outside the library would not be protected by the gate; the fallback is then plain linear regression. Finally, relative error divides by $|\\\\gate|\\\\ge0.5$ and is capped at 2, so cases with small effects, or estimators with heavy-tailed errors, are summarized differently from absolute error.\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{Limitations.}\"\ns=s.replace(old2,new2,1)\nopen('paper.tex','w').write(s)\nE\n$TMPDIR/venv/bin/python build_figures.py >/dev/null && cp paper.tex $TMPDIR/build/ && cd $TMPDIR/build && python3 - <<'E'\ns=open('paper.tex').read()\ns=s.replace(\"\\\\clearpage\\n\\\\bibliography{references}\",\"\\\\label{bodyend}\\\\clearpage\\n\\\\bibliography{references}\")\nopen('paper.tex','w').write(s)\nE\ncd $TMPDIR/build; cp /workspace/paper/figures/* figures/ 2>/dev/null; pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode paper.tex >/dev/null; bibtex paper >/dev/null; pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode paper.tex >/dev/null; pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode paper.tex | grep -E \"undefined|Output written\"; grep bodyend paper.aux | cut -c1-60"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3130,"cache_read_input_tokens":176486,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3130},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"96cff137-170e-47bc-8fe8-44e05d15df83","uuid":"76731bcb-ae51-4bc6-82aa-2d570e71d3f8","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:26:33.712Z","request_id":"req_011CgvpesinNKfRpxpVHrnCM","tool_use_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GBc8jHBpb33cEFWThQSfYm","display_name":"Bash"}]}
